          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Confucius</strong><br>\nConfucius, born in 551 BCE in the state of Lu, now known as Shandong province, came from a modest family background. His father passed away when Confucius was just three years old, leaving the family in a state of poverty. Despite these hardships, he showed a strong passion for learning and knowledge from an early age, which set the foundation for his later teachings and philosophies.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Philosophy and Teachings</strong><br>\nConfucius's teachings focused on morality, social relationships, justice, and sincerity. He believed that these values were crucial for creating a harmonious society. His philosophy emphasized the importance of personal and governmental morality, and he advocated for strong family loyalty, ancestor worship, respect of elders by their children, and the family as a basis for an ideal government.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Analects</strong><br>\nThe Analects are a collection of Confucius's sayings and ideas compiled by his disciples after his death. This text remains a primary source of Confucianism and has been studied for centuries. It encapsulates his wisdom on various subjects and offers insights into his thoughts on ethics, leadership, and education.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
