          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Influence on Chinese Society</strong><br>\nConfucius's ideas greatly influenced Chinese society and government. His philosophy became the official imperial philosophy in China, affecting educational, social, and political norms. His emphasis on education and the moral development of individuals has left a lasting imprint on Chinese culture and East Asian life.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Confucianism as a Religion</strong><br>\nWhile Confucianism is not a religion but a philosophical and ethical system, over time it has taken on religious overtones. It prescribes a set of practical rules for daily life and has built rituals around these teachings. Confucian temples and the worship of Confucius as a sage and teacher show the deep respect for his teachings.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Criticism and Challenges</strong><br>\nThroughout history, Confucius's ideas have faced criticism and challenges. During certain periods, such as the Cultural Revolution, his ideas were rejected and criticized for being feudal or out of date. However, his philosophies have endured and continue to be a subject of interest and respect in modern times.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
